# Compared to Markdown

@lede
A construct-by-construct comparison. The differences are intentional;
the rationales accumulate.
@end

## Headings

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
# H1
## H2

H1 with setext
==============

H2 with setext
--------------
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
# H1
## H2
```
@end
@end

Setext headings are *not* in Brief. The two surface forms (`#` and
`===`/`---`) produce the same AST in Markdown; one form, by Brief's
rule, is the legal one. The `#`-prefixed form wins because it scales to
six levels.

## Emphasis

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
**bold**, *italic*, ***both***
__bold__, _italic_, ___both___
~~strikethrough~~
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
*bold*, _italic_, *_both_*
~strike~
```
@end
@end

Markdown spends both `*` and `_` on the bold/italic distinction; Brief
spends one marker per emphasis kind. There is no `***both***` shortcut —
nest two emphasis runs explicitly.

## Lists

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
- one
* two
+ three

1. one
3. three
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
- one
- two
- three

1. one
2. two
```
@end
@end

Brief refuses `*` and `+` as bullet markers. Out-of-order numbering is a
compile error, not a "the renderer figures it out" affordance.

## Tables

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
| Name | Age |
|------|-----|
| Ada  | 30  |
| Bob  | 25  |
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
@t
| Name | Age
| Ada | 30
| Bob | 25
```
@end
@end

Brief drops the separator row. The trailing `|` is optional. Column
alignment moves to a directive argument: `@t(align: [left, right])`.

## Links

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
[GitHub](https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief)

[my reference][gh]
[gh]: https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
@link[GitHub](https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief)
```
@end
@end

Inline links are a shortcode. There is no reference-style link form —
Brief is context-free, and reference-style links require a multi-pass
parser for one feature.

## Images

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
![architecture diagram](diagram.png)
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
@image(src: "diagram.png", alt: "architecture diagram")[]
```
@end
@end

More verbose by token count. Worth the cost: the alt text is a *named
argument*, not "everything between `![` and `]`."

## Inline code and backticks

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
Use the `printf` function.
Run ``echo `whoami` `` for the user.
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
Use the `printf` function.
Run ``echo `whoami` `` for the user.
```
@end
@end

Identical. The double-backtick escape for embedded backticks is the only
exception to Brief's "one canonical form" rule.

## Code blocks

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
    indented four spaces

```fenced
fenced code
```

~~~~
or fenced with tildes
~~~~
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
```fenced
fenced code
```
```
@end
@end

Brief refuses the indented and tilde forms. The triple-backtick fence is
the only legal form. Minification attributes (`@minify`, `@nominify`,
`@minify-keep-comments`) extend the opening fence.

## Callouts

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown — GFM")
```markdown
> [!WARNING]
> Watch out for this.
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
@callout(kind: warning)
Watch out for this.
@end
```
@end
@end

The Brief form is two extra characters per callout, but the `kind` is
typed and validated against the enum — `note`, `tip`, `important`,
`warning`, `caution`. A typo is @ec[B0404], not silent passthrough.

## Inline HTML

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
<sub>2</sub>, <sup>2</sup>, <kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd>
<details>
<summary>Click</summary>
content
</details>
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
@sub[2], @sup[2], @kbd[Ctrl+C]
@details(summary: "Click")
content
@end
```
@end
@end

The four most common inline HTML use cases are built-in shortcodes.
Anything else means the document shouldn't be HTML in the first place;
if you must, register a custom shortcode in `brief.toml`.

## Frontmatter

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown — Jekyll")
```markdown
---
title: My document
date: 2026-04-29
tags: [foo, bar]
---

# Document content
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
+++
title = "My document"
date = 2026-04-29
tags = ["foo", "bar"]
+++

# Document content
```
@end
@end

`+++` delimiters and TOML inside, not `---` and YAML. TOML matches
`brief.toml`, has a single canonical parser per language, and produces
clean error messages.

## Task lists

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown — GFM")
```markdown
- [x] done
- [ ] todo
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
- [x] done
- [ ] todo
```
@end
@end

Identical syntax. The marker is exact in Brief — `[X]`, `[  ]`, or
`[x]` without the trailing space is plain text, not a typo'd task.

## Errors

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```text
(silent: renders as best as the parser can guess)
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```text
error[B0204]: emphasis cannot nest with the same marker
  --> doc.brf:14:23
   |
14 | This is *outer *inner* outer*.
   |        ^^^^^^^ inner emphasis re-uses the `*` marker
```
@end
@end

This is the durable difference. Markdown silences ambiguity; Brief
points at it.
